name markdawn

source {
    url "git@github.com:necessary-nu/markdawn.git"
    ref main
}

children (
    {
        id markdawn-highlighter-boundary
        title "Establish the renderer-neutral syntax-highlighting boundary"
        priority 0
        implements {
            main ([spec:mw:req:highlighting.api] [spec:mw:req:highlighting.span-semantics] [spec:mw:req:highlighting.error-fallback])
        }
        log (
            {
                at 2026-07-29T12:50:59.623254Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-07-29T19:22:36.683666Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Scope widened by the operator to include migrating the grammar backend from arborium to ntreesitter (../ntreesitter, described as a saner port of arborium). Rationale: this node already owns highlighting.arborium-transition, and the renderer-neutral SyntaxHighlighter / SyntaxSpan / HighlightError seam it establishes is exactly the boundary a backend swap goes through — doing the swap as a separate node would mean touching every renderer twice. Footprint at the time of this decision: 26 first-party arborium call sites (src/highlight.rs, src/lib.rs, src/tracey/render.rs, crates/markdawn-android/{lib,grammar_store}.rs), 104 generated markdawn-swift-lang-* crates which are gitignored and produced by crates/markdawn-swift/generate-plugins.sh (so the template is the real edit, not 104 crates), plus the highlight / highlight-all-languages cargo features and the path dependency on ../arborium. ntreesitter is structured differently — nine crates including -plugin, -provider, -catalog, -runtime and -wire — so the plugin registration protocol spec'd as ffi.plugin.grammar-registration (mw_lang_&lt;name&gt;_init(RegisterFn)) is expected to change shape rather than just names. A read-only API survey was commissioned before any code change; plan the migration from its findings. Note this node is claimed by a different session, so the scope change was recorded here rather than acted on unilaterally."
            }

            {
                at 2026-07-29T19:27:32.101127Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Read-only API survey of ntreesitter vs arborium, findings for planning the backend swap.\n\nBLOCKER — grammar coverage. arborium ships 104 grammars; ntreesitter-catalog ships 11 (c, cpp, html, javascript, json, lean, markdown, rust, silo, tsx, typescript). 93 missing, including bash, python, go, java, ruby, php, swift, kotlin, css, yaml, toml, sql. imports/inventory.json shows a deliberate staged import from arborium at revision 2d7ac7f1 with blake3-pinned provenance, so this is a backlog rather than an oversight — but the import cadence is not determinable from the repos and no schedule is credible until it is answered.\n\nPLUGIN PROTOCOL IS A REDESIGN, NOT A RENAME. Per-language `mw_lang_<name>_init(RegisterFn)` becomes one universal `ntreesitter_provider_v1()` returning a repr(C) descriptor; the callback handoff inverts (host pulls a descriptor rather than the plugin calling mw_register_grammar, which has no counterpart). The single-symbol model breaks the current loader design — you cannot dlsym the same symbol from 104 cdylibs without per-handle resolution, so the Swift and JNI loaders need reworking. Descriptor surface is much wider than the current template emits: aliases, extensions, exact filenames, injection targets, parser ABI and provenance hashes, none of which exist in arborium's per-language crates. ntreesitter defines the ABI but ships no dynamic loader — that stays markdawn's job.\n\nNO HTML EMITTER. arborium::advanced::spans_to_html has no ntreesitter equivalent; markdawn must own a span-to-HTML serializer (src/highlight.rs:118-127).\n\nSPAN SEMANTICS — one real divergence. Byte offsets, half-open ranges, raw capture names and nesting all survive. But `priority` is NOT arborium's `pattern_index`: it is a monotonic emission-order counter assigned after the upstream engine resolves query precedence, not highlights.scm line order. Losing captures are never surfaced, so re-deciding equal-range captures by query order is gone rather than renamed. Partially-overlapping non-nested spans cannot occur (strict stack), making the word \"overlapping\" in highlighting.span-semantics inaccurate.\n\nOTHER DESIGN DECISIONS NEEDED. Registry construction is fallible and rejects ambiguous aliases at build time, where arborium tolerated collisions — with 104 merged alias sets, collisions are likely and unpredictable until descriptors exist. DynamicRegistry hands out immutable generation-tracked snapshots, so VdomPipeline pinning a store at construction will silently not see late-registered languages. HighlightOutput carries a new `diagnostics` vec for non-fatal partial-injection failures that the current boundary cannot express. ntreesitter vendors tree-sitter 0.26.8 with links = \"tree-sitter\" and pins tree-sitter-language =0.1.7, while markdawn's swift lang crates depend on 0.1 — check for a links collision early.\n\nSPEC RULES NEEDING REWRITE: highlighting.span-semantics (priority and overlapping wording), uikit.syntax.span-priority (names pattern_index explicitly), highlighting.arborium-transition (retire or generalise to provider-transition), highlighting.api (needs a diagnostics clause). uikit.syntax.capture-fallback and span-bounds are unaffected.\n\nBUILD: ntreesitter generates parsers at build time from grammar.json + scanner.c with blake3 verification rather than shipping ~100 MB of pre-generated static data, so build time rises and artifact size falls, per-crate. Feature story maps cleanly (catalog has one feature per language, default all). wasm is first-class and spec'd, unlike arborium."
            }

            {
                at 2026-07-29T22:56:18.029406Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Operator has parked the arborium-to-ntreesitter migration: ntreesitter is still being built out and is not relevant to markdawn right now. This supersedes the scope widening recorded earlier — the backend swap is NOT active work on this node. What remains in scope here is the original renderer-neutral SyntaxHighlighter / SyntaxSpan / HighlightError seam, which is exactly the right thing to finish regardless: it is what lets the backend be swapped later without touching every renderer again. Keep arborium behind the seam. The survey findings recorded above stay valid as preparation and should be re-read rather than re-derived when the migration is picked up; the gating fact is that ntreesitter's catalog covers 11 languages against arborium's 104, with a tooled but per-file-curated import (xtask import / catalog / normalization / metadata, 230 provenance-tracked entries for those 11). Do not re-open the migration until the operator raises it."
            }

            {
                at 2026-07-30T00:05:42.437719Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Second ntreesitter analysis, after the catalog grew from 11 to 104 languages. Verdict changed from blocked to ready-with-caveats, and the blocker moved.\n\nRESOLVED SINCE THE FIRST SURVEY. Grammar parity is real, not scaffolded: 104 dirs in languages/, all 104 in the catalog's features, DESCRIPTORS and EXPECTED_IDS, set-equal to markdawn's 104 markdawn-swift-lang-* names with zero diff either way and no renames. They build — 104 libnts_lang_*.rlib, 327 parser archives, and a catalog test asserting all 104 form one Registry. pattern_index parity is genuine and was fixed below the API: tree-sitter-highlight/src/highlight.rs computes match_.pattern_index minus the layer's highlights_pattern_index, i.e. the zero-based index within the highlights query, which is exactly arborium's number, threaded out through a new HighlightEvent::HighlightStart field. The plugin ABI objection is disproven: load_provider_v1_from_handle takes a caller-supplied resolver so the caller dlopens and dlsyms, and there is a test (identical_entry_names_are_resolved_per_library_handle) showing N libraries exporting the same symbol resolve per handle.\n\nTHE NEW BLOCKER IS A CARGO links COLLISION, verified empirically with throwaway crates rather than reasoned about. ntreesitter-runtime is package name tree-sitter with links = \"tree-sitter\"; arborium-tree-sitter declares the same links value. Three results: both as plain deps fails; both as MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE optional deps still fails, because links uniqueness is checked over the declared graph before feature resolution; and ntreesitter alone without a [patch.crates-io] table also fails, because ntreesitter-highlight declares tree-sitter-highlight = \"=0.26.8\" from crates.io which drags in crates.io tree-sitter. Consequence: arborium and ntreesitter cannot coexist in one Cargo graph at any feature setting, so there is no bisectable intermediate and the migration is a single all-or-nothing commit across ~7 workstreams. This also makes [spec:mw:req:highlighting.arborium-transition] unimplementable as written — its premise is that markdawn retains a legacy arborium feature alongside the new one.\n\nUPSTREAM REQUESTS, in priority order. (A) Rename the runtime's links value to something namespaced and drop the workspace [patch.crates-io] requirement — markdawn cannot work around this because [patch] is only honoured in the workspace-root manifest, so markdawn would have to copy ntreesitter's private-git patch table into its own root, applying to all 113 of its crates, and keep it in sync forever, and it still would not allow arborium for even one commit. (B) Re-export ntreesitter_plugin from ntreesitter behind a feature, since otherwise consumers need two git deps to the same private repo with hand-matched revs, multiplied by 104 generated plugin crates. (C) Ship the per-language cdylib plugin crates or a cargo xtask generate-plugins, because the alternative is 104 individual git deps plus markdawn re-deriving ntreesitter's canonical IDs from directory layout, which means every language ntreesitter adds silently breaks markdawn's generator. (D) impl GrammarStore for Arc<Registry> — GrammarStore::language returns a borrow tied to &self, which is why DynamicRegistry structurally cannot be a store; markdawn's workaround (snapshot per highlight call, an RwLock read plus an Arc clone) is cheap, so this is lower priority. (E) HighlightOutput::into_spans for consumers whose boundary cannot carry non-fatal diagnostics.\n\nMARKDAWN-SIDE WORK, in dependency order: own a span-to-HTML serializer (~30 lines; ntreesitter deliberately has no emitter and should not gain one, since markdawn's output is <a-s> custom elements feeding its own sanitiser, not generic span class); replace the blanket HighlightSpans adapter with a concrete highlighter holding Arc<DynamicRegistry>; drop pub use arborium from lib.rs and tracey/render.rs, which is what actually leaks the backend; rewrite both grammar_store.rs files, dropping the 9-argument mw_register_grammar shim since plugins now carry their own descriptors; rewrite generate-plugins.sh around export_provider_v1!; update the root manifest. Steps must land together.\n\nSPEC IMPACT: highlighting.arborium-transition delete or replace; uikit.syntax.span-priority drop the equal-range tie-break clause (ntreesitter emits only the winning capture and its own spec forbids consumers re-running precedence, so markdawn's tie-breaks are both dead code and a contradiction); highlighting.span-semantics amend the priority wording; highlighting.api relax &mut self to &self and note it is renderer-neutral but not store-neutral; highlighting.error-fallback add that non-fatal diagnostics are dropped; and a new rule is needed for the span-to-HTML serialization markdawn is about to own.\n\nHONEST GAPS: query CONTENT was not diffed against arborium, so coverage parity is established but output equivalence is not — each language carries provenance/arborium.yaml suggesting mechanical import, but that is inference. Wasm was not built, so the 104-language footprint against markdawn's ~100 MB warning is unmeasured. Whether request A has a hidden cost in the Swift/Android link step is unknown. And ntreesitter's own delivery state is unclear — commit 0b59c76 is an errata reopening incomplete delivery and a ntreesitter-legacy-retirement node exists, so whether the operator considers it done is worth asking rather than inferring."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id parsing
        title "Markdown parsing"
        priority 1
        children (
