            {
                id defects.uikit-syntax-logic-apple-bound
                title "Pure span logic is Apple-bound only by its return type, blocking host tests"
                priority 2
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-29T22:39:08.401994Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "uikit.syntax.capture-fallback, uikit.syntax.span-bounds and uikit.syntax.span-priority are pure data transformations — dotted-prefix capture-name fallback, UTF-8 to UTF-16 offset conversion with clamping, and a widest-first span sort. None of them needs Apple runtime objects to compute. They are untestable on this host only because capture_default_color returns Retained&lt;UIColor&gt; and the sort and bounds logic is inlined into build_highlighted, inside a subtree gated on cfg(target_os = \"ios\"). Extracting a platform-neutral capture_color_name(&amp;str) -&gt; Option&lt;&amp;str&gt;, the span-ordering comparator, and utf16_offset into a module that is not cfg-gated would make all three host-testable and would let the same logic be shared with any future non-Apple renderer that consumes spans. This is a refactor rather than a test gap, which is why it was filed separately instead of being done under defects.test-coverage-gaps. Worth noting span-bounds is the clamping rule — the review that first surfaced the uikit gaps called it \"the sharpest gap\" precisely because stale spans after an edit are a real failure mode and nothing verifies the clamp."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-29T23:57:50.982215Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

