            {
                id defects.iced-rtl-selection-rect-per-glyph
                title "Base-RTL selection paints one rectangle per glyph instead of per run"
                priority 2
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-29T23:55:46.973227Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while closing defects.iced-bidi-doc-misattached, verified with real headless cosmic-text layout rather than inferred.\n\npaint_bidi_selection feeds layout_runs() glyphs straight into group_in_selection_glyphs, whose adjacency test computes next.x_left - run.x_right and so presumes glyph boxes ascend in x. That holds only when the line's base direction is LTR. LayoutRun::glyphs is ordered along the line's own reading direction — logical order per bidi run, runs sequenced along the base direction — so x ascends iff !run.rtl. On a base-RTL line the precondition is violated, two abutting boxes read as separated by the sum of their advances, coalescing never fires, and grouping degenerates to one rectangle per glyph: אבג gives 3 glyphs and 3 rects, אבג abc gives 7 and 7, the same with niqqud gives 11 and 11.\n\nImportantly this is a decomposition defect, not a wrong-pixels defect — that was checked, not assumed. The per-glyph rects tile the selected extent exactly: no gaps, no overlap, summed widths equal rightmost minus leftmost. It is not the \"one contiguous rectangle swallowing unselected text\" failure mode.\n\nWhat it does cost: it violates iced.paint.bidi-runs' own MUST of one rectangle per visually contiguous run; it multiplies fill_quad calls by glyph count on RTL text; and with the translucent 0.35-alpha fill at non-integer coordinates, abutting quads are a plausible source of visible seams at every glyph boundary — that last one is a risk rather than a confirmed artifact, since it needs a real surface to observe. Negative-width rects are reachable in principle if a merge happened under descending order, but effectively unreachable because the merge needs both advances to sum under 0.5px; the worst observed real case is a zero-width rect from a combining mark.\n\nFix is one line of intent — sort glyph boxes by x_left before grouping, or compare against the facing edge — but it changes rect counts, so the existing grouping fixtures move with it and iced.paint.glyph-run-grouping (now v1) would likely need another bump."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id defects.iced-duplicates-span-logic-divergently
                title "iced reimplements capture fallback, priority and bounds, two of them wrongly"
                priority 1
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-29T23:57:40.498607Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found while extracting the platform-neutral span logic for defects.uikit-syntax-logic-apple-bound. src/adapter/iced/render.rs reimplements all three transformations that now live in src/highlight.rs, and two of the three disagree with the spec the UIKit path is held to.\n\n1. scope_color (render.rs:1693-1709) does scope.split('.').next() — it takes only the FIRST dotted component rather than walking successively shorter prefixes. So `string.escape` collapses to `string` in iced while resolving to its own Escape accent on UIKit. It also carries a second hardcoded palette, so the two adapters can drift in colour as well as in resolution.\n\n2. highlight_to_spans (render.rs:1663-1681) resolves priority as an innermost-wins plus higher-pattern_index-wins selection loop — the inverse formulation of order_spans' widest-first sort. Same intent, opposite construction, so they can disagree on ties.\n\n3. The same function clamps out-of-range offsets with .min(len) at render.rs:1648-1649 and :1665-1666, where uikit.syntax.span-bounds requires DISCARDING an out-of-range span. So a stale span after an edit renders clamped-and-styled in iced and unstyled in UIKit — the divergence is user-visible, not cosmetic.\n\nFiled P1 rather than P2 because (3) is a spec conformance difference between two shipping renderers, not a tidiness issue. The fix is for the iced path to consume SyntaxAccent + capture_accent + order_spans + utf16_range so there is one capture vocabulary, one priority policy and one bounds policy. Note that would also let the three uikit.syntax.* rules be restated as renderer-neutral rules rather than UIKit-specific ones, since the logic is no longer Apple-bound — worth deciding at the same time."
                    }
                )
            }
        )
    }

