    {
        id ntreesitter
        title "Migrate the highlighting backend from arborium to ntreesitter"
        children (
            {
                id ntreesitter.spec
                title "Retire the arborium transition rule and spec the ntreesitter provider binding"
                priority 0
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T15:02:04.882805Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Rule work in docs/spec/markdawn/. Downstream nodes claim these rules; this node authors them and carries no implements of its own.\n\nRETIRE. [spec:mw:req:highlighting.arborium-transition] goes. It is premised on markdawn \"retaining its legacy Arborium feature\", which the links collision makes impossible — there is no configuration in which both backends are present. Removing it orphans the ref on markdawn-highlighter-boundary.implements and the three /test annotations in src/highlight.rs, all of which must go in the same change. Replace with a provider-neutral successor covering the same ground: the backend adapts to SyntaxHighlighter without exposing its span or error types to renderers, preserving every span field and the provider diagnostic.\n\nREVISE. [spec:mw:req:highlighting.span-semantics] — the July 29 survey claimed priority was an emission counter and that overlapping-but-not-nested spans were impossible. Both are now wrong: ntreesitter's priority is the zero-based highlights-query index, the same quantity as arborium's pattern_index. Re-read the rule against the current engine before changing a word of it; it may need no change at all. [spec:mw:req:highlighting.api] may need a clause for HighlightOutput.diagnostics, which carries non-fatal partial-injection failures the current boundary cannot express.\n\nADD. Registry composition (static catalog vs empty-plus-dynamic), dynamic registration semantics (snapshot-per-render, and the consequence that a pipeline pinning a snapshot at construction will not see later registrations), and delegation of span-to-HTML to the provider formatter with no renderer-side precedence resolution.\n\nCASCADE — NOT BOOKKEEPING. [spec:mw:def:ffi.plugin.grammar-registration] specifies markdawn's nine-argument mw_register_grammar handoff, where the plugin calls into the host. That inverts: the host resolves nts_init_<canonical_id> and pulls a descriptor. The rule body changes, so it versions to +1, and the bindings node (P3, currently Done) goes Stale and needs re-annotation at its new sites. Expect that transition rather than being surprised by it at sync.\n\nUNAFFECTED. uikit.syntax.capture-fallback, uikit.syntax.span-bounds and uikit.sem:span-priority are span algebra over markdawn's own SyntaxSpan and do not move."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T17:21:38.615041Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T17:26:38.185147Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Spec landed. Four decisions worth the record.\n\nSPAN-SEMANTICS NEEDED NO CHANGE. The July 29 survey called it inaccurate on two counts and both are wrong. Its \"query-pattern priority\" clause is accurate because ntreesitter's Span.priority is the zero-based index within the highlights query — arborium's pattern_index, not the emission counter the survey described. Its \"overlapping and nested spans\" clause is a preservation requirement on the boundary (the seam must not merge or drop them), not a claim that any backend emits partial overlaps; tree-sitter-highlight is a stack machine and emits strictly nested events either way, which does not make the requirement false. Left at v0. uikit.syntax.span-priority likewise names pattern_index, which is markdawn's own SyntaxSpan field and unaffected.\n\nDIAGNOSTICS AS A NEW RULE, NOT A BUMP TO highlighting.api. ntreesitter's HighlightOutput carries a diagnostics vec for non-fatal failures — an injected language that would not resolve, an exhausted injection budget — which the boundary's Result<Vec<SyntaxSpan>, HighlightError> cannot express. Dropping it would contradict a rule already in the spec: highlighting.error-fallback exists so the boundary keeps the diagnostic \"instead of turning failure into absence\", and an Ok-with-degradation is exactly that case. Rather than widen highlighting.api's return type — a breaking change to every SyntaxHighlighter impl — the new highlighting.diagnostics requires only that the information stay reachable, satisfiable by an additive defaulted trait method. highlighting.api stays at v0 and its cascade never fires.\n\nLATE-REGISTRATION IS SPEC'D BECAUSE IT IS A SILENT FAILURE. DynamicRegistry hands out immutable generation-tracked snapshots and does not itself impl GrammarStore, so a pipeline that pins a snapshot at construction compiles, runs, and quietly never highlights a language registered afterwards. That reads in the field as \"highlighting randomly doesn't work\". The rule states the observable requirement and leaves snapshot-per-render as one conforming implementation.\n\nDELIBERATE UNCOVERED AND STALE STATE. Five new highlighting.* rules are uncovered and stay that way until ntreesitter.core-binding; ffi.plugin.grammar-registration is bumped to +1 and its six impl and three test refs in the Swift and Android loaders are stale until those nodes land. Both are the intended signal, not debt. The arborium blanket impl in src/highlight.rs is now deliberately unannotated rather than re-pointed at highlighting.provider-binding: that rule also forbids re-exporting the backend crate, which src/lib.rs still does, so claiming it there would be a false claim."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T17:27:00.400868Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ntreesitter.output-parity
                title "Diff highlight output across all 104 languages while arborium still builds"
                priority 0
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T15:01:45.842417Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Operator chose full 104-language coverage over a bounded representative sample. Rationale: a bounded sample cannot catch a regression in a long-tail grammar, and markdawn has no test for most of the catalog, so the sample would have been chosen by convenience rather than risk.\n\nWHY IT MUST RUN FIRST. links = \"tree-sitter\" makes a single-process A/B comparison impossible, so this is two dumps diffed offline: one binary per repo emitting (start, end, capture, pattern_index) per fixture, compared by canonical id. Once ntreesitter.core-binding lands, arborium no longer builds in this workspace and the baseline is unobtainable. This node is the last moment the comparison exists.\n\nWHAT COUNTS AS SIGNAL. Coverage parity is already established — 104 languages, set-equal names, zero diff, all building — and each language carries provenance/arborium.yaml suggesting a mechanical import. Query CONTENT has never been diffed, so output equivalence is unestablished. Expect one known, intentional class of difference: ntreesitter's HTML path deliberately omits arborium's pattern-index dedup step, because the engine resolves query precedence before spans are emitted and highlight.html-output forbids renderers re-running it. Differences of that shape are expected and should be characterised, not treated as regressions; anything else is a real finding.\n\nFREE SIDE-EFFECT. This node builds every language on both sides, so it also yields the plugin dylib size comparison that ntreesitter.plugin-split-upstream wants a number for."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T17:28:11.447939Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T17:59:10.297029Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "RESULT. 104 languages, 166 fixtures, both backends. ntreesitter highlighted 166 of 166; arborium 156 of 166. Comparison is three-layer because a raw span diff badly over-reports.\n\nLAYER 1 — RAW SPANS. 32 fixtures identical, 3 same-spans-different-order, 121 different. Reading that as a regression would have been wrong.\n\nLAYER 2 — GEOMETRY, duplicates collapsed. arborium emits one span per matching query pattern; ntreesitter emits one per (range, capture), keeping the highest pattern index. Collapsing that, 43 fixtures are geometrically identical. Pattern-index VALUES differ on 6 of those — the inherited-query languages renumber, e.g. hlsl emits 53/83/102 where ntreesitter emits 5/24 for the same ranges and captures — but relative order within a range is preserved on every single one, so markdawn's tie-break is unaffected. Zero fixtures reorder ties.\n\nLAYER 3 — WHAT MARKDAWN ACTUALLY PAINTS. Simulating order_spans (widest-first, then start, then ascending pattern_index, last-wins-per-byte): 122 of 156 fixtures paint IDENTICALLY. The 6,000-span \"loss\" is almost entirely losing captures markdawn was always going to paint over. 34 fixtures paint differently; 10 of those by more than 5% of styled bytes — objc/RCTLog.mm 39%, scss/_buttons.scss 42%, markdown/rust-readme.md 39%, r/detect.R 17%, vue/App.vue 16%, cobol 11%, silo 10%, scala 7%, both gleam fixtures 7%. Caveat on this layer: order_spans is the UIKit model. The HTML path resolves through spans_to_html and iced has its own (divergent) logic — see defects.iced-duplicates-span-logic-divergently — so identical-paint is strong evidence, not proof, for those two renderers.\n\nMIGRATION FIXES THREE LANGUAGES. fsharp, haskell and perl fail on arborium with \"unsupported language\" across all 10 of its failures; ntreesitter highlights all of them (fsharp 45-1772 spans, haskell up to 1298, perl up to 145). The arborium message is a misattribution: crates/arborium/src/store.rs try_lang! ends in CompiledGrammar::new(config).ok(), so a query-compilation failure becomes None and is reported as an unsupported language. These three are in arborium's all-languages feature list and in markdawn's 104 generated plugin crates, so markdawn ships them today as silently unhighlightable.\n\nFIVE INJECTION GRAMMARS ARE ABSENT FROM THE CATALOG. 33 diagnostics: jsdoc (javascript, typescript, tsx, vue, html), regex (javascript, typescript, vim, query), comment (vim, haskell, groovy, fsharp), phpdoc (php), pod (perl). Not in either catalog, but ntreesitter surfaces the failure where arborium was silent — which is highlighting.diagnostics earning its place on the first run.\n\nWHERE PAINT DIFFERS, THREE CLASSES. (1) Injected HTML is coarser: in markdown/rust-readme.md arborium splits media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" into attribute + string, ntreesitter emits one attribute span over the whole thing, so the value loses its string colour. (2) scss comments resolve to @spell rather than @comment; markdawn's capture_accent maps spell to no accent, so // comments render unstyled. (3) Assorted generic/specific swaps, some improvements (variable to variable.parameter, 768 bytes; function.call to method).\n\nVERDICT. Does not block the swap. Rendering is unchanged on 78% of the corpus and the changes are query-content differences per language, which is what an unaudited import predicts. The scss comment-to-spell case is the one I would call a plain regression and is worth a fixture-level bug upstream. Recorded rather than fixed here: this node's contract was to obtain the baseline before it becomes unobtainable, and it has.\n\nMETHOD. examples/arborium_span_dump.rs in this repo (deleted by ntreesitter.retire-arborium); the ntreesitter half and the three analysis scripts are deliberately outside both repositories, in the session scratchpad, since the comparison is a one-time gate and not tooling either project should carry. Both walk fixtures recursively — a flat walk silently skips silo, whose corpus nests under fixtures/corpus/, while still reporting success."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T17:59:39.786541Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T22:25:10.431711Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "RE-RUN against ntreesitter 77bd99b (decorative-capture filter in the shipped registry). Baseline arborium dump unchanged and reused.\n\nMY HEADLINE METRIC WAS WRONG AND HID THE RESULT. \"Identical painted output\" went 122 to 114 fixtures, i.e. it reported the fix as a regression. It cannot distinguish improvement from degradation, because a capture markdawn maps to no accent renders exactly like unstyled text: arborium painting @spell and ntreesitter painting @comment is a fix that a raw capture comparison scores as a difference. Re-measured through markdawn's own capture_accent — the dotted-prefix walk in src/highlight.rs — classifying each differing byte as gained, lost or changed colour.\n\n  before errata: gained 179, lost 4020, changed 510\n  after errata:  gained 12153, lost 4020, changed 510\n\nThe errata coloured 11,974 additional bytes and lost nothing. Clean win. Dominant gains: spell to comment 9646 bytes, spell to string 1895, nospell to keyword.directive 408.\n\nA PRE-EXISTING MARKDAWN DEFECT, NOT A MIGRATION RISK. Those 12 KB are unstyled in markdawn TODAY. arborium emits @comment and @spell as separate spans with the same pattern index, and order_spans breaks that tie by emission order, so @spell paints last and wins; capture_accent(\"spell\") is None, so scss, ini, asciidoc, zig, prolog, sql, query, wit, asm and capnp comments render as plain text on the UIKit path right now. The migration fixes it. Worth its own defect node if the swap slips.\n\nREMAINING 4,020 LOST BYTES SPLIT TWO WAYS, and only one is ntreesitter's.\n  (a) markdawn's accent table does not know ntreesitter's capture vocabulary: function.definition to definition.function (170 bytes), type.definition to definition.type (75), comment to constructor (78), plus a tail of _function, _scope and parameter. exact_capture_accent has no arm for definition.*, constructor or parameter, so they resolve to no accent. That is markdawn-side work and belongs in ntreesitter.core-binding — extend the table when the adapter lands, or these render plain.\n  (b) ntreesitter emits nothing where arborium emitted something: type 1224, comment 692, string 594, keyword 323, tag 152, property 113 — 3,098 bytes. Consistent with the five injection grammars absent from the catalog plus per-language query granularity. Not resolved here.\n\nCHANGED (510 bytes) is dominated by string to attribute, 473 — the injected-HTML granularity case, reported upstream with byte offsets from markdown/rust-readme.md.\n\nLEFTOVER. crates/ntreesitter-highlight/src/lib.rs:551 still calls is_decorative inside TestStore::new under #[cfg(test)] at line 526. Harmless — the production path at registry/mod.rs:195 is now correct and is what these numbers measure — but it is a second copy of the rule, which is what the errata set out to remove."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ntreesitter.plugin-split-upstream
                title "Split ntreesitter-plugin so provider exports do not link the runtime"
                priority 0
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T15:01:14.707466Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "External dependency on the ntreesitter repository, modelled here because markdawn's plugin-crates node cannot start until it lands.\n\nREQUEST. ntreesitter-plugin depended on ntreesitter for decode.rs alone — the host half that registers a validated descriptor. export.rs, which is all a provider library uses, needs only ntreesitter-provider. Left coupled, all 104 markdawn-swift-lang-* cdylibs would link ntreesitter -> ntreesitter-highlight -> ntreesitter-runtime through an edge they never call, inheriting the links = \"tree-sitter\" identity. Two costs: every iOS plugin dylib carries the Tree-sitter runtime (whether the linker drops it is unmeasured, not known), and the plugin crates fall inside the core swap's non-bisectable commit instead of landing ahead of it.\n\nSTATUS AT PLANNING TIME. Taken up and in flight, uncommitted in the ntreesitter working tree at c9e6ed4: a new crates/ntreesitter-abi carries abi.rs + export.rs and the export_provider! macro, depending on ntreesitter-provider alone; ntreesitter-plugin retains decode.rs + loader.rs and keeps the ntreesitter dep. That is exactly the shape this node asked for. Close it once the split is committed and a provider cdylib built against ntreesitter-abi is confirmed free of the runtime in its dependency graph."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T17:20:38.629683Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Satisfied upstream at ntreesitter a23a56a, tree clean. Verified empirically rather than from the commit message: `cargo tree -p ntreesitter-abi` is ntreesitter-abi -> ntreesitter-provider -> tree-sitter-language and nothing else, and `cargo tree -p nts-lang-rust` reaches the same three plus a build-only ntreesitter-build. Neither touches ntreesitter-runtime, so neither inherits links = \"tree-sitter\".\n\nDelivered stronger than requested. The ABI types, the export encoder and export_provider! moved to ntreesitter-abi; decode and loader stayed in ntreesitter-plugin, which now re-exports the ABI so a host still writes one import. Beyond that they pinned the invariant as [spec:nts:req:plugin.export-independence] with project-check enforcing the export crate's dependency set — so a future edge back to the runtime fails their gate rather than silently re-coupling markdawn's 104 cdylibs.\n\nConsequence for this plan: ntreesitter.plugin-crates is now genuinely free of the core swap and lands as its own earlier commit. markdawn's generated crates take ntreesitter-abi, not ntreesitter-plugin — the loaders take ntreesitter-plugin.\n\nUNCHANGED. ntreesitter-runtime is still package.name = \"tree-sitter\" with links = \"tree-sitter\", so the ntreesitter.core-binding / swift-loader / android-loader commit remains atomic."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T17:20:45.172193Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ntreesitter.plugin-crates
                title "Regenerate the 104 plugin cdylibs against nts-lang providers"
                priority 0
                deps (ntreesitter.spec ntreesitter.plugin-split-upstream)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T15:02:21.836159Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Lands as its own commit AHEAD of the core swap: with the ntreesitter-abi split these crates depend on ntreesitter-abi + ntreesitter-provider only, reach no links crate, and so coexist with arborium.\n\nGENERATOR. crates/markdawn-swift/generate-plugins.sh currently discovers languages by scanning ../arborium/langs/<group>/<name>/crate — directory layout as a source of truth, which is why adding a language upstream could silently break generation. Drive it off the `all` feature list in ntreesitter-catalog's Cargo.toml instead: that list is declared, canonical, and exactly the 104 ids.\n\nPER-CRATE SHAPE. Each generated crate becomes a dep on nts-lang-<name> plus ntreesitter-abi, and a single export_provider!(nts_init_<sanitized>, nts_lang_<sanitized>::DESCRIPTOR). This deletes markdawn's hand-written nine-argument unsafe registration ABI and the LazyLock-vs-&str query-borrowing workaround the arborium template needed.\n\nSYMBOL NAMING IS UNCHANGED IN KIND. nts_init_<canonical_id> with dashes normalised to underscores — the same sanitisation the current mw_lang_<name>_init template already does, so c-sharp still yields nts_init_c_sharp. Because the symbol is per-language rather than shared, process-global dlsym keeps working and neither loader's contract changes; use ntreesitter_abi::init_symbol to derive the name rather than formatting it by hand in two places.\n\nDESCRIPTOR SURFACE IS WIDER THAN THE ARBORIUM TEMPLATE EMITTED. Aliases, file extensions, exact filenames, injection targets, parser ABI and provenance hashes now travel with the grammar instead of being inferred from the crate name. That is the point — it is what lets the registry reject ambiguous aliases at build time — but registry construction is fallible, and with 104 merged alias sets a collision is plausible and will not be visible until the descriptors are all present. Build the full registry once, early, rather than discovering it in the loaders."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T23:36:18.986157Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T01:51:23.46939Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ntreesitter.core-binding
                title "Bind ntreesitter behind the highlighter seam and drop the arborium re-export"
                priority 0
                implements {
                    main (
                        [spec:mw:req:highlighting.provider-binding]
                        [spec:mw:req:highlighting.diagnostics]
                        [spec:mw:req:highlighting.registry-composition]
                        [spec:mw:req:highlighting.late-registration]
                        [spec:mw:req:highlighting.html-delegation]
                    )
                }
                deps (ntreesitter.spec ntreesitter.output-parity ntreesitter.plugin-split-upstream)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T15:01:31.257785Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "ATOMICITY. This node, ntreesitter.swift-loader and ntreesitter.android-loader land as ONE commit. They are siblings rather than a dep chain because none precedes the others — they are simultaneous. The cause is links = \"tree-sitter\", declared by both arborium-tree-sitter and ntreesitter-runtime: the moment markdawn's arborium dep is replaced, no crate in the workspace may reach arborium, and both binding crates reference markdawn::arborium::GrammarStore, so all three break together or land together. Commit with nplan_commit ids: [ntreesitter.core-binding, ntreesitter.swift-loader, ntreesitter.android-loader].\n\nThe 104 plugin crates are NOT in this commit — with the ntreesitter-abi split they depend only on ntreesitter-provider, reach no links crate, and land ahead of it.\n\nSCOPE. Cargo.toml: drop the arborium path dep, add ntreesitter + ntreesitter-catalog (default-features = false, since catalog defaults to all) + ntreesitter-highlight; highlight = catalog with no languages, highlight-all-languages = catalog/all. src/highlight.rs: replace the HighlightSpans blanket impl and the two arborium From impls with the ntreesitter adapter (Span.priority maps to SyntaxSpan.pattern_index, same zero-based highlights-query index); spans_to_html delegates to ntreesitter_highlight::spans_to_html. src/lib.rs: delete `pub use arborium;` and expose a markdawn-owned Highlighter instead — do not re-export the backend, that re-export is what made this migration touch every consumer. Then src/tracey/render.rs:218 and examples cli.rs:34, iced_demo.rs:84, html_snapshot.rs:50.\n\nTWO API SHAPES NEEDED. Static: ntreesitter_catalog::register_all(RegistryBuilder) -> Registry, which impls GrammarStore. Dynamic (Swift/Android, zero grammars baked in): DynamicRegistry does NOT impl GrammarStore — only Registry and RegistrySnapshot do — so the dynamic path holds a DynamicRegistry globally and takes .snapshot() per render. That is a deliberate design (explicit reader generations), not an omission, but it is a different shape from arborium's shared Arc<GrammarStore> and both loaders change accordingly."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ntreesitter.swift-loader
                title "Move the Swift grammar store and loader onto the ntreesitter registry"
                priority 0
                deps (ntreesitter.spec ntreesitter.plugin-split-upstream)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T15:02:47.311859Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Lands in the same commit as ntreesitter.core-binding and ntreesitter.android-loader — see the decision note there.\n\ncrates/markdawn-swift/src/grammar_store.rs takes the same treatment as the Android store: DynamicRegistry in place of Arc<GrammarStore>, descriptor validation via ntreesitter_plugin::load_provider in place of the nine-argument mw_register_grammar. The two files are near-verbatim ports of each other today and should stay that way; if the shared logic is worth extracting, that is a separate node, not a smuggled one.\n\nuikit.rs:18 imports markdawn::arborium::Highlighter and constructs it at line 34 from the global store — that becomes the markdawn-owned Highlighter over a snapshot.\n\ncrates/markdawn-swift/include/markdawn.h declares mw_register_grammar for the Swift side to take the address of. That declaration goes; ntreesitter ships include/ntreesitter/provider.h for the descriptor struct. The three Markdawn.xcframework/*/Headers/markdawn.h copies are build outputs and must be regenerated, not hand-edited.\n\nSwift's loader hands over a function pointer today because the plugin calls back into the host. That inverts — the host resolves nts_init_<id> and pulls a descriptor — so the Swift-side handoff simplifies rather than merely renaming."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ntreesitter.android-loader
                title "Move the Android grammar store and JNI loader onto the ntreesitter registry"
                priority 0
                deps (ntreesitter.spec ntreesitter.plugin-split-upstream)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T15:02:35.602779Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Lands in the same commit as ntreesitter.core-binding and ntreesitter.swift-loader — see the decision note there.\n\ncrates/markdawn-android/src/grammar_store.rs: replace the OnceLock<Arc<GrammarStore>> with a DynamicRegistry, and mw_register_grammar's nine-argument body with descriptor validation through ntreesitter_plugin::load_provider. The five return codes collapse: the non-UTF-8 arms disappear with the unpacked string arguments, leaving symbol-missing and invalid-descriptor.\n\nlib.rs:215 register_language keeps dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT, ...) — per-language nts_init_<id> symbols mean no dlopen handle and no JNI signature change. Only the symbol name and the call shape change: resolve, then hand the entry point to load_provider with the canonical id.\n\nlib.rs:85 constructs its Highlighter from the store; that becomes a per-render .snapshot(). Note the pinning hazard — VdomPipeline holding a snapshot taken at construction will not observe languages registered afterwards, which is a behaviour change from the shared Arc and is exactly the kind of thing that looks like \"highlighting randomly doesn't work\" in the field.\n\nlib.rs:674-680 builds a CompiledGrammar from arborium_rust in test code, and crates/markdawn-android/Cargo.toml carries the arborium-rust dep that feeds it. Both go; nts-lang-rust replaces them."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ntreesitter.retire-arborium
                title "Assert arborium is gone from the dependency graph and refresh the docs"
                priority 0
                deps (
                    ntreesitter.plugin-crates
                    ntreesitter.core-binding
                    ntreesitter.swift-loader
                    ntreesitter.android-loader
                )
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-30T15:03:03.951924Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Closes the migration. The gate is mechanical: `cargo tree` over the workspace with every headless-safe feature must show no arborium crate and no crates.io tree-sitter, which is the only proof that the links collision is actually resolved rather than merely dormant behind a feature that happens to be off.\n\nThen the full declared check suite from .config/nplan/config.styx — fmt, clippy -D warnings, nextest across the workspace — at the feature set those commands already carry. Run `nplan check` in a terminal first so the commit gate consumes the witnesses instead of re-running the suite inside the 60s budget.\n\nDocs: README's workspace layout is already stale (see defects.readme-workspace-layout-stale, which lists one crate of about 110) and the 104 plugin crates change name-for-name in this migration, so the two should be reconciled together rather than leaving a second wrong list behind. The arborium checkout at ../arborium stays on disk as reference; nothing in this repo may reach it."
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-07-30T15:00:38.875333Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Operator green-lit the arborium-to-ntreesitter migration on 2026-07-30, superseding the park recorded on markdawn-highlighter-boundary. Upstream state at planning time (ntreesitter @ 78b9601):\n\nRESOLVED SINCE THE JULY 29 SURVEY. (1) Grammar parity: 104 languages, set-equal to markdawn's 104 markdawn-swift-lang-* names, zero diff, all building. (2) pattern_index parity: fixed below the API as match_.pattern_index.saturating_sub(layer.config.highlights_pattern_index), i.e. the zero-based index within the highlights query — arborium's number. Span.priority is that value, not the emission counter the first survey described. (3) The [patch.crates-io] requirement is gone: tree-sitter-highlight is a vendored path dep at crates/tree-sitter-highlight. (4) commit 49fa940 ships ntreesitter_highlight::spans_to_html with ThemeSlot tag and class names byte-identical to arborium's, so markdawn does NOT need to own an HTML formatter and existing theme CSS keeps working. (5) commit 899848c replaces the single ntreesitter_provider_v1 symbol with per-language nts_init_<canonical_id> (dashes to underscores) and drops V1 branding throughout; process-global dlsym still works, so neither the Swift loader contract nor the JNI loadLanguage signature has to change.\n\nSTILL OPEN. ntreesitter-runtime is still package.name = \"tree-sitter\" with links = \"tree-sitter\", the same value arborium-tree-sitter declares, so arborium and ntreesitter cannot coexist in one Cargo graph at any feature setting — links uniqueness is checked over the declared graph before feature resolution. That forces one non-bisectable commit; see the note on ntreesitter.core-binding for its exact extent.\n\nDEPENDENCY FORM. ntreesitter crates are all publish = false, so markdawn takes path deps on ../ntreesitter exactly as it does on ../arborium today. This does not newly block publishing markdawn — it already could not be published with a path dep on arborium."
            }

            {
                at 2026-07-30T23:39:38.794345Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "SCOPE CORRECTION from the operator, 2026-07-31: coexistence is not the plan; arborium is replaced.\n\nI had let a property of the tooling become a planning goal. Once the ntreesitter-abi split landed, the 104 plugin crates no longer reached a links crate, so they *could* have landed in their own commit ahead of the core swap — and I structured plugin-crates around that. But being able to hold both backends in one graph for one commit is worth nothing when the intent is to delete one of them. It buys a bisection point on a boundary nobody will bisect across, at the cost of a transient state that has to be reasoned about and reviewed.\n\nCONSEQUENCE. plugin-crates, core-binding, swift-loader and android-loader are now siblings on [spec, output-parity, plugin-split-upstream] rather than a chain, and land as ONE commit that removes arborium. retire-arborium depends on all four and stays separate, because verifying the dependency graph is clean and refreshing the docs is genuinely after-the-fact work.\n\nWHAT DOES NOT CHANGE. The generated plugin crates still take ntreesitter-abi rather than ntreesitter-plugin. That choice was only ever half about the links identity; the other half is that a provider library has no reason to link the Tree-sitter runtime for a decode path it never calls, across 104 iOS dylibs. The host takes ntreesitter-plugin, a provider takes ntreesitter-abi, and that split is correct on its own terms.\n\nALSO IN THE REPLACEMENT COMMIT. examples/arborium_span_dump.rs, which was scheduled for retire-arborium — it depends on arborium and cannot outlive it. Its baseline is already captured and analysed on ntreesitter.output-parity."
            }
        )
    }
)
